{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c048f461-7770-4ed7-a623-0ee7c0b85d81","agentId":"a1bd5e47851999369","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Independent adversarial review of bats-lang/bridge PR 279, head dd45827de6ca55aa37a3e13490d74deba856a679, for ticket bats-lang/bridge#267 (book CSS: the outside-read proof, book container opcodes 13 to 18 and the confinement). Kind: NEW CAPABILITY (bridge atoms/opcodes). CI on this head is green (check/check, gate, text-lint, canonical-files).\nRead bridge's CLAUDE.md fully first (sections \"Primitives only\", \"Every outcome is distinct\", \"An atom's JS only writes the answer down\", \"Adversarial review before merge\": the three new-capability questions) and the ticket with all its comments (its Done-when, word for word, is the standard). Read the PR body (Plan, Execution, Evidence, Deviations) and the whole diff. A previous round rejected an earlier head because BOOK_REFUSED was a hand-copied list; this head generates `const BOOK_REFUSED` in emit_js_book from css's published table (css 2026.11.9.83875: `book_refused_count ()` and `book_refused_entry`), adds css to bats.toml, raises the book bounds by 1019, adds a test that ties the generated list to css's table, and moves .github/repository-version to e9cbf5ff (which holds that css archive). Your job is the whole PR, with the new commits (b5782a3, dd45827) in particular. Answer with evidence (file:line, CI job names): (1) is there no way to do this with what exists; (2) is each atom/opcode the minimal 1:1 wrapper; (3) every DOM write is an opcode of the diff stream; (4) the overlap rule: old forms keep their signatures and bounds (produce_bridge, produce_bridge_app, emit_js_all) and a test calls them (tests/dynamic/old-bridge); the new forms are beside them; the p2 ticket removing old forms exists if the PR says it does (bridge#280 is named in the body: check it exists and is p2); (5) the refusal list in bridge JS is a safety refusal generated from css's table, not app policy; the JS decides nothing beyond that and writes answers down; (6) no .md/.rst line added, no `$UNSAFE` or `g1ofg0` or runtime assert added in the new commits; the staload of css/src/book.sats is justified; (7) the generated-list test would fail if css's table and the JS list differed (say how you know). Post nothing. Reply with Verdict (approved or changes needed), `Kind:` and a short `Checked:` list (plain ASCII sentences, no code fences, no HTML), notes with evidence, and the full head SHA you reviewed."},"uuid":"521bfbd0-cd70-4bec-8df6-f0f4e6eb8682","timestamp":"2026-11-10T00:41:49.096Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_projects","cwd":"/home/user","sessionId":"da1996b1-7fe2-5742-a481-c46311328875","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"structured-riding-bird"}
{"parentUuid":"16b66b2d-a16b-4e05-8d7b-f020eec0c5db","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a1bd5e47851999369","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgtKZcHa3Aw8nDAZ1Bogk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5437,"cache_read_input_tokens":137618,"output_tokens":3876,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":503},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5437},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3876,"cache_read_input_tokens":137618,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5437,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5437,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":4449,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"db77d1bd-3a11-4dbd-bab8-7ac0ce06cb7c","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgtKZbzhzNw8TdvkA1Tov","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"5m","attributionAgent":"reviewer","attributionMcpServer":"github","attributionMcpTool":"get_job_logs","type":"assistant","uuid":"0d556a5d-45fd-4465-b44d-aca7ce9ed969","timestamp":"2026-11-10T00:43:42.205Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_projects","cwd":"/home/user","sessionId":"da1996b1-7fe2-5742-a481-c46311328875","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"structured-riding-bird"}
{"parentUuid":"0d556a5d-45fd-4465-b44d-aca7ce9ed969","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a1bd5e47851999369","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgtKZcHa3Aw8nDAZ1Bogk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NioDxtogFq4AFfnQn1NoMR","name":"SubagentHandback","input":{"message":"Verdict: approved\n\nKind: NEW CAPABILITY (bridge atoms and opcodes). The kind holds from the diff: opcodes 13 to 18, `book_stream`/`dom_flush_book`, `book_font_add`, and the `_outside` atom twins are all additions.\n\nHead reviewed: dd45827de6ca55aa37a3e13490d74deba856a679\nI posted nothing. I ran nothing locally. My evidence is the diff, the files at the head and CI.\n\nChecked:\n- Ticket bridge#267 Done-when, item by item against the PR body and CI. Fixtures, dynamic cases, unchanged `old-bridge` and the three review questions are all present.\n- CI at this head: check / check, check / layout, gate, text-lint / lint and canonical-files / canonical-files are success. check / c-runtime is skipped. One earlier text-lint run was cancelled and superseded by a later success.\n- The `check / check` log (job 129519450948), step `tests/dynamic/run.sh`, prints `ok   book`, `ok   outside` and `ok   old-bridge`, with no FAIL.\n- The new commits b5782a3 and dd45827 read in full.\n- No `.md` or `.rst` is in the 48 changed files.\n\nEvidence for the questions:\n\n(1) Nothing existing does this.\n- `dom_flush` takes any byte array, so a proof demanded in dom or css alone could be bypassed. `infile`, `dblob` and `file_idb_*` carry no program-versus-outside distinction.\n- Ordinary `SET_ATTR` cannot set containment that the app cannot later remove, and cannot tie an element to an outside root. It would also let the ordinary operations forge `data-book-*`.\n- One sheet opcode cannot carry a sheet larger than the stream buffer, hence the pieces (17) plus an end (18).\n- The PR body table answers this per capability.\n\n(2) The atoms are minimal wrappers.\n- Each `_outside` atom in `src/outside.bats` is a twin of an existing atom with a stronger type (`infile_outside`, `dblob_outside`, `OUTSIDE_READ(cs)`) and a separate JS table or store.\n- `OUTSIDE_READ` is minted in one place, the `extern praxi outside_read_made` in the `$UNSAFE` region of `outside.bats`. The ticket asks for this.\n- Opcodes 13 to 18 and `book_font_add` are one operation each. `book_font_add` maps 1:1 to `FontFace` plus `document.fonts.add`, and JS makes the face name `book-<cid>-<n>`.\n- `emit_js_book` (`src/book_js.bats`) honours 13 to 18 only when the flush is the book flush. Through `dom_flush` they are logged as refused, and the case `book-container` plays this.\n\n(3) DOM writes go through the stream.\n- Every element or attribute write is an opcode: 13 to 16 create the container and elements and set the attributes. Opcode 18 adopts the sheet into `document.adoptedStyleSheets` inside the flush, so it is ordered with the elements.\n- `batsJsBookFontAdd` is an atom. It touches `document.fonts`, not the element tree, so it is not a DOM write.\n- `batsDomFlush` is replaced by a wrapper that walks the stream and hands the unchecked runs to the original flush. The ordinary operations that can carry `style` or `data-book-*` (SET_ATTR, REMOVE_ATTR, CLONE_NODE, and CREATE_ELEMENT for `style`) are checked there.\n\n(4) The overlap rule holds.\n- The PR has 2860 additions and 1 deletion. The deletion is the `.github/repository-version` line, so no existing line of `src/` is changed.\n- The old forms `produce_bridge`, `produce_bridge_app` and `emit_js_all` keep their bounds (119271 / 121271).\n- The new forms are beside them: `produce_bridge_book` (143363), `produce_bridge_app_book` (145363) and `emit_js_all_book` (143363). The bound changes in b5782a3 touch only these.\n- `tests/dynamic/old-bridge/src/bin/gen.bats` calls `produce_bridge` and is unchanged. It passes in CI (`ok   old-bridge`). `tests/static/accept/produce-bridge-old-bound` still exists. The accept fixture `produce-bridge-book-bound` calls the old and the book forms with their exact room.\n- bridge#280 exists, is open and is labelled p2. The PR body does not name it, so nothing hangs on the PR's own claim. #280 is about the outside atoms' answer forms, not about removing old forms. This PR changes no signature, so no old-form removal ticket is required.\n\n(5) The refused list is a safety refusal generated from css, not app policy.\n- `emit_js_book` writes `const BOOK_REFUSED = [` and then calls `put_refused_entries(b, $CB.book_refused_count (), 0)`. That loops `book_refused_entry(i)` over css's table, with termination metric `.<cnt - i>.`.\n- css's getter is typed `book_refused_entry {i:nat | i < 17} (i: int i): [m:int | 1 <= m; m <= 11] string(m)` and `book_refused_count (): int 17`. That proves the index and the 70-byte-per-entry room statically, with no runtime check.\n- No entry text is copied into bridge.\n- If css's table grows to 18 or shrinks to 16, the type-check fails at the call or at the getter.\n- `bookTextRefused` and `bookSheetOk` read that one constant. The list is css's `book_refused` list (url(, image-set(, @import, @font-face, !important, backslash, `<`, `/*` and the rest).\n- The rest of the JS refuses by structure and writes the refusal to `console.error`. That covers the container-prefix selector check, `@media`/`@supports` only, the brace depth, the face and sheet size caps, and nesting. All of it is confinement the ticket asks for in §3.2, not app policy. No app choice (which attributes, roles or defaults) is made in the JS.\n\n(6) Hygiene of the new commits.\n- b5782a3 adds no `$UNSAFE`, `g1ofg0`, `assert` or runtime bounds check. `g1u2i(string1_length(entry))` and `char2int1` are plain conversions of a typed string.\n- The only `$UNSAFE` and `praxi` in the PR are in `src/outside.bats`, from the earlier rounds. That is the single mint point the ticket asks for, and bridge is `unsafe = true`.\n- The staload is justified. css's `lib.bats` does not load `book.bats` (per the PR body), so the table is reachable only as `staload CB = \"css/src/book.sats\"`.\n- `#use css as C` is there to fetch the package, and the alias is not otherwise used.\n- css's dependencies are builder, array, arith and str. It does not depend on bridge, so there is no cycle.\n- `bats.lock` adds css 2026.11.9.83875 (with str). dd45827 moves `.github/repository-version` to e9cbf5ff, whose `css/css_2026.11.9.83875.bats` archive exists (I fetched it).\n\n(7) The generated-list test would fail on a difference.\n- `tests/dynamic/book/src/bin/gen.bats` writes css's `book_refused_count ()` and every `book_refused_entry(i)` to `dist/refused.txt`, through a loop separate from the emitter's.\n- `tests/dynamic/book/check.mjs` (new block before `const boot`) extracts the `BOOK_REFUSED` of `dist/bridge.js` by regex and evaluates it.\n- It then throws if the count differs from the number of entries. It also throws if `JSON.stringify(list)` differs from `JSON.stringify(table)`, which covers order, content, a dropped or extra entry, and a mangled character or escape.\n- It prints `refused list: 17 entries, those of css's table` as the first line of `expected`. `run.sh` diffs the output against that file, and CI passes (`ok   book`).\n- The test is an equality of two readings of one css table, not an independent oracle. It catches faults in the emitter (escaping, U+FFFD, order, truncation), and the types catch a count change. If css adds an entry, the lists stay equal, which is the point of generating them. The fixture lines in `check.mjs` still play each of the 17 entries individually.\n\nNotes (not blocking):\n- The outside atoms fold distinct failures into one constructor and swallow throws. For example, `NotFetchedOutside` covers refused scheme, refused final URL, network failure and thrown error, and `batsJsFetchOutside` has `catch (e) {}`. This departs from bridge's CLAUDE.md sections \"Every outcome is distinct\" and \"An atom's JS only writes the answer down\". The ticket's Done-when does not require answer forms, and the earlier review already filed bridge#280 (p2) for exactly this. I accept it as a follow-up. The PR body says \"Deviations: none\" and does not cite #280, so adding that reference to the body would be more honest.\n- The CLAUDE.md sections \"book content\" and \"outside read\" are not added. The PR body says why, and the ticket's \"What\" list does ask for them. The reasons are in the keyword comments at the top of `src/outside.bats` and `src/book_content.bats`. A follow-up that may add `.md` lines has to land them.\n- The browser behaviour of constructable style sheets and `FontFace` is stood in for in jsdom, as the PR body says. `check.mjs` shows what the bridge asks of the browser, not what a browser does with it.\n\nReviewed head: dd45827de6ca55aa37a3e13490d74deba856a679"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5437,"cache_read_input_tokens":137618,"output_tokens":3876,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":503},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5437},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3876,"cache_read_input_tokens":137618,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5437,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5437,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":22987,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01NioDxtogFq4AFfnQn1NoMR":{"message":"Verdict: approved\n\nKind: NEW CAPABILITY (bridge atoms and opcodes). The kind holds from the diff: opcodes 13 to 18, `book_stream`/`dom_flush_book`, `book_font_add`, and the `_outside` atom twins are all additions.\n\nH